# Hover action rail implementation checklist

## Goal

Implement the reusable hover action rail described in
`Docs/hover-action-rail-proposal.md`, then migrate the native Hauling
minecart-stop zoom action from permanent right-side buttons to one contextual
rail that appears to the left of the hovered stop row.

The result must preserve the existing route selection, stop centering,
highlighting, marker rendering, native input, and cleanup behavior while
eliminating overlap with the native scrollbar.

## Confirmed design decisions

- The production module is
  `src/scripts_modinstalled/dwarfui/widgets/hover_action_rail.lua`.
- `HoverActionTarget`, `HoverAction`, and `HoverActionRail` are proper
  `defclass` classes.
- The generic widget contains no Hauling, minecart, route, stop, map, or
  `AssetButton` dependency.
- One rail owns one stable set of action widgets; it does not allocate one
  action set per visible target.
- The full-parent controller remains transparent.
- A moving internal `widgets.Panel` owns the visible background, border,
  inset, and action widgets.
- Background, border, and inset are independently configurable.
- Target and action coordinates use rail-local space.
- Hover presentation never grants activation authority; every action validates
  its target immediately before execution.
- The Hauling rail is placed only to the left of the native panel.
- Route-header rows never produce a stop-action target.
- The first action remains nearest the native row and later actions grow
  outward.
- Input outside the visible rail passes through unchanged.
- The current per-visible-row minecart action pool will not remain as a second
  production placement system.
- Every new or modified class and method receives complete LuaDoc comments.
- Unit tests use the `name.spec.lua` format; live component tests end only in
  `.ds.lua`.

## Phase 0: Add the generic public types and module contract

- [x] Add `dwarfui/widgets/hover_action_rail.lua` under the production module
      tree.
- [x] Define `HoverActionTarget` as a proper `defclass`.
- [x] Accept only a stable nonempty string or integer target key.
- [x] Validate that target anchors contain integer coordinates with
      `x2 >= x1` and `y2 >= y1`.
- [x] Copy the anchor table at construction so later consumer mutation cannot
      change an existing target snapshot.
- [x] Retain target payload as opaque consumer-owned data without interpreting
      it in the generic module.
- [x] Define `HoverAction` as a proper `defclass`.
- [x] Validate its nonempty stable action ID, widget factory, activation
      callback, visibility callback, enabled callback, and nonnegative gap.
- [x] Provide documented default visibility and enabled callbacks that return
      `true`.
- [x] Define `HoverActionRail` as a proper `widgets.Widget` subclass.
- [x] Declare and validate the required target, validation, context, pointer,
      and placement-bound providers.
- [x] Copy and validate the ordered action list and reject duplicate action
      IDs.
- [x] Validate placement names, action gaps, scroll-consumption configuration,
      background values, border values, and content inset values.
- [x] Create every action widget exactly once by passing a rail-owned
      activation function to its factory.
- [x] Reject factories that do not return a `widgets.Widget`.
- [x] Ensure action callbacks capture the owning rail and action definition,
      never a target snapshot.
- [x] Export all three classes from the module.
- [x] Add full LuaDoc class, field, parameter, return, and method contracts.
- [x] Register the module with the DwarfUI module registry and reload order.
- [x] Add the module and exported classes to package-contract coverage.
- [x] Add `tests/hover_action_rail.spec.lua` construction and validation tests.

### Phase 0 completion gate

- [x] All three exported types are proper classes with validated contracts.
- [x] One rail instance constructs each configured action widget exactly once.
- [x] The module loads without importing any consumer-specific module.
- [x] Focused class, registry, and package-contract tests pass.

## Phase 1: Implement the configurable rail surface

- [x] Create one internal `widgets.Panel` surface during rail initialization.
- [x] Keep the controller full-parent and visually transparent.
- [x] Attach all action widgets to the internal surface, not directly to the
      full-parent controller.
- [x] Support `background_pen=false` for a fully transparent surface.
- [x] Support a static parsed background pen.
- [x] Support a target-sensitive background callback.
- [x] Support `border_style=false` for a frameless surface.
- [x] Support standard DFHack frame styles such as `gui.FRAME_THIN`,
      `gui.FRAME_INTERIOR`, and `gui.FRAME_INTERIOR_MEDIUM`.
- [x] Support custom `gui.Frame` definitions with consumer-provided edge,
      corner, and pen configuration.
- [x] Support a target-sensitive border callback.
- [x] Support scalar and per-edge content insets.
- [x] Default to a transparent background, no border, and zero inset.
- [x] Resolve target-sensitive presentation only from the current target.
- [x] Apply the resolved background to the surface's `frame_background`.
- [x] Apply the resolved border to the surface's `frame_style`.
- [x] Apply the content inset to the surface's `frame_inset`.
- [x] Render the background before the border and actions.
- [x] Keep action coordinates body-relative inside the surface.
- [x] Evaluate each action's dynamic visibility and enabled state whenever its
      target changes.
- [x] Compact visible actions without reconstructing hidden action widgets.
- [x] Hide the surface and every action widget when the rail has no target.
- [x] Include background, border, inset, and action-gap cells in the rail's
      visible ownership rectangle.
- [x] Add focused tests for transparent, opaque, `keep_lower`, and
      target-sensitive backgrounds.
- [x] Add focused tests for frameless, standard, custom, and target-sensitive
      borders.
- [x] Add focused tests for scalar and asymmetric insets.
- [x] Prove the full-parent controller never paints outside the moving surface.
- [x] Prove widgets are created once and reused across presentation changes.

### Phase 1 completion gate

- [x] Consumers can configure background, border, and inset independently.
- [x] The visible surface paints only its own bounds.
- [x] Background, border, and action render order is deterministic.
- [x] Presentation changes reuse the same action-widget instances.
- [x] Focused surface and rendering tests pass.

## Phase 2: Implement deterministic placement geometry

- [x] Convert the screen pointer to rail-local coordinates before target
      resolution.
- [x] Require target anchors and placement bounds in the same local coordinate
      space.
- [x] Measure only currently visible action widgets.
- [x] Include each widget's current dimensions, action gap, content inset, and
      outer surface edges in the measured bounds.
- [x] Implement `left` placement with the first action nearest the target and
      later actions growing leftward.
- [x] Implement `right` placement with the first action nearest the target and
      later actions growing rightward.
- [x] Implement a centered horizontal action sequence for `above` placement.
- [x] Implement a centered horizontal action sequence for `below` placement.
- [x] Vertically center left and right rails against their target anchor.
- [x] Try configured placements in deterministic declaration order.
- [x] Accept a placement only when the complete outer surface fits within the
      current placement bounds.
- [x] Never clamp a surface across its target to force it to fit.
- [x] Hide the rail when no configured placement fits.
- [x] Store the complete outer surface as `rail_bounds`.
- [x] Compute the rectangular retention bridge between target and surface when
      a configured gap separates them.
- [x] Recompute placement after target-anchor, action-visibility, widget-size,
      inset, border, placement-bound, or parent-layout changes.
- [x] Add focused tests for every placement and mixed action dimensions.
- [x] Add tests proving action order remains stable as more actions are added.
- [x] Add boundary tests at every screen edge and with a nonzero parent origin.
- [x] Add tests for placement fallback order and the no-fit hidden result.
- [x] Add tests proving border and inset dimensions affect fitting and
      centering.
- [x] Add tests for zero-gap and nonzero-gap bridge geometry.

### Phase 2 completion gate

- [x] Every supported placement produces exact deterministic action and
      surface bounds.
- [x] The primary action remains nearest the target for left and right rails.
- [x] No accepted placement crosses the target or placement boundary.
- [x] Dynamic presentation changes cause correct remeasurement without widget
      recreation.
- [x] Focused placement tests pass.

## Phase 3: Implement hover ownership and lifecycle

- [ ] Implement hidden, target-hover, and rail-hover states without a timeout
      or delayed latch.
- [ ] Sample `context_active()` before resolving or retaining a target.
- [ ] Clear immediately when the host context is inactive.
- [ ] Resolve at most one target from each pointer sample.
- [ ] Rebind immediately when the pointer crosses directly from one target to
      another.
- [ ] Treat a matching key as the same logical target while accepting its
      refreshed anchor and payload.
- [ ] Validate an already retained target during each hover update.
- [ ] Clear when retained-target validation fails.
- [ ] Retain the target while the pointer remains inside its refreshed anchor.
- [ ] Retain the target while the pointer is inside `rail_bounds`.
- [ ] Retain the target while the pointer is inside the target-to-rail bridge.
- [ ] Clear immediately after the pointer leaves target, bridge, and rail.
- [ ] Run hover updating before both rendering and input dispatch.
- [ ] Recompute child layout before rendering after a target or style change.
- [ ] Make `get_target()` explicitly return the current presentation snapshot,
      not an activation-authoritative result.
- [ ] Make `clear()` hide the surface and actions and release target, bridge,
      geometry, and dynamic-state references.
- [ ] Ensure ordinary overlay destruction is sufficient cleanup.
- [ ] Do not create a screen, global hook, timeout, singleton, or registry.
- [ ] Ensure reload creates a new widget generation with no callback retaining
      the old rail or target.
- [ ] Add state-transition tests covering target entry, target switching,
      rail entry, bridge entry, target return, and complete leave.
- [ ] Add tests for context loss, target disappearance, anchor movement,
      parent resize, and explicit clear.
- [ ] Add a regression proving target-to-rail pointer movement does not hide
      the action before it can be clicked.

### Phase 3 completion gate

- [ ] The rail follows exactly one current target and never requires a hover
      delay.
- [ ] Pointer movement from a target into its rail preserves action access.
- [ ] Context loss and stale targets clear all transient state immediately.
- [ ] No global or screen-owned lifecycle state is introduced.
- [ ] Focused hover and lifecycle tests pass.

## Phase 4: Implement input ownership and activation freshness

- [ ] Refresh hover state at the beginning of `onInput()`.
- [ ] Dispatch input to visible action subviews before handling rail
      background input.
- [ ] Revalidate the active target immediately before every activation.
- [ ] Require the validated target to retain the same stable key.
- [ ] Replace the presentation snapshot with the fresh validated target before
      invoking the action.
- [ ] Pass only the freshly validated target to the action callback.
- [ ] Never invoke an action when validation fails.
- [ ] Clear a stale target after rejecting its activation.
- [ ] Consume a stale click that landed on a previously owned action so it
      cannot reach the map.
- [ ] Consume successful action clicks.
- [ ] Consume clicks on visible rail background, border, inset, and gaps.
- [ ] Prevent disabled actions from invoking their callbacks.
- [ ] Keep disabled-action surface clicks owned by the rail so they cannot
      reach the backing map.
- [ ] Consume scroll-wheel input over the visible rail when
      `consume_scroll=true`.
- [ ] Pass scroll-wheel input through when `consume_scroll=false`.
- [ ] Pass native target-row clicks through unchanged.
- [ ] Pass pointer and keyboard input outside the visible rail unchanged.
- [ ] Ensure a hidden rail consumes no input.
- [ ] Define and test the action callback's success and rejection return
      behavior independently from click ownership.
- [ ] Add focused tests for valid activation, callback rejection, stale keys,
      changed payloads, changed anchors, and disappearing targets.
- [ ] Add exact hit tests for action, background, border, inset, gap, bridge,
      target, and outside cells.
- [ ] Add scroll, keyboard, disabled-action, hidden-rail, and pass-through
      regressions.

### Phase 4 completion gate

- [ ] No action can execute against a presentation-only or stale target.
- [ ] Every visible rail cell has deterministic input ownership.
- [ ] Native targets and unrelated input retain pass-through behavior.
- [ ] Scroll consumption follows configuration exactly.
- [ ] Focused input and activation tests pass.

## Phase 5: Migrate the Hauling stop action to one hover rail

- [ ] Import the generic hover-action-rail module from the production
      minecart-route overlay.
- [ ] Build the recenter action as a generic `HoverAction` whose factory
      returns the production `AssetButton`.
- [ ] Preserve the complete vanilla 3-by-3 `STOCKS_RECENTER` graphic and
      classic fallback.
- [ ] Preserve normal and hover pens that do not overwrite their configured
      rail background.
- [ ] Add one `HoverActionRail` instance to the overlay after the marker and
      label subviews so the rail renders last.
- [ ] Configure the Hauling rail with `placement_order={'left'}`.
- [ ] Configure its initial background, frame style, and inset explicitly
      instead of relying on generic defaults.
- [ ] Keep the rail background and border constants outside the generic module.
- [ ] Resolve targets only while the production focus includes
      `dwarfmode/Hauling`.
- [ ] Resolve targets only from current cached native panel bounds,
      `scroll_position`, `view_routes`, and `view_stops`.
- [ ] Make the entire three-row stop entry hoverable across the native list
      content width.
- [ ] Exclude the native scrollbar and cells outside the list from target
      resolution.
- [ ] Return no target for route headers, empty rows, clipped rows, or missing
      stops.
- [ ] Use a stable composite route-and-stop key.
- [ ] Store route ID, stop ID, flattened row index, current anchor, and copied
      stop position data in the opaque payload.
- [ ] Revalidate current focus, cached bounds, scroll position, flattened row,
      route ID, stop ID, anchor, and stop position before activation.
- [ ] End the complete outer rail surface immediately before the native
      panel's left edge.
- [ ] Vertically center the recenter action against its hovered three-row stop.
- [ ] Ensure the rail surface, background, border, and actions never overlap
      the native scrollbar.
- [ ] Preserve button activation that selects the owning hauling route,
      centers the map, and highlights the current stop.
- [ ] Preserve selection-indicator and in-world route-marker rendering.
- [ ] Preserve the open Hauling menu, native route data, and list scroll
      position after zoom activation.
- [ ] Consume wheel input over the rail so it cannot change the map z-level.
- [ ] Leave wheel input over the native list to native DF and rebind or hide
      the rail on the following update.
- [ ] Clear the rail on menu closure, focus change, overlay disable, world
      unload, missing Hauling state, and reload.
- [ ] Remove the production per-visible-row action layout and pool after the
      rail migration has no remaining caller.
- [ ] Remove or replace obsolete registry entries, package contracts, and unit
      tests instead of retaining two placement implementations.
- [ ] Retain only domain adapter code that remains necessary for current-row
      resolution and activation.
- [ ] Update focused minecart overlay tests for hover-only presentation,
      left-side geometry, styling, route selection, zoom behavior, scrolling,
      stale-target rejection, and cleanup.
- [ ] Add a second synthetic action test proving later actions grow outward
      without changing target-resolution code.

### Phase 5 completion gate

- [ ] No permanent stop-row buttons or right-side action pool remains active.
- [ ] Hovering a real stop produces one styled left-side rail; hovering a route
      header produces none.
- [ ] The rail never overlaps the native panel or scrollbar.
- [ ] Zoom activation still selects, centers, highlights, and consumes input
      correctly.
- [ ] Scrolling and native edits cannot leave an action bound to an old stop.
- [ ] Focused Hauling integration tests pass.

## Phase 6: Prove the interaction against the native Hauling menu

- [ ] Extend the existing minecart component file whose filename ends only in
      `.ds.lua`.
- [ ] Keep the prepared-save requirement explicit: Premium graphics, a visible
      route header, visible stops at distinct tiles, and a scrollable list.
- [ ] Open the native Hauling menu from the component test.
- [ ] Exercise the registered production overlay, production
      `HoverActionRail`, and production `AssetButton`.
- [ ] Use actual cached panel bounds and actual flattened native row state.
- [ ] Use DwarfSpec's standard pointer API for every pointer move.
- [ ] Move the pointer over a visible real route header and prove no rail
      appears.
- [ ] Move the pointer over the number, label, and remaining content cells of
      a visible real stop entry and prove the same target rail appears.
- [ ] Assert exactly one rail surface and one recenter action set are visible.
- [ ] Assert the complete outer rail surface lies left of the native panel and
      does not intersect the scrollbar.
- [ ] Read rendered background cells and compare them with the configured
      production background pen.
- [ ] Read rendered border cells and compare their glyphs, tiles, and pens with
      the configured production frame.
- [ ] Read all nine rendered `STOCKS_RECENTER` graphics cells.
- [ ] Move the pointer from the native stop row across the surface border and
      onto the actual zoom button without losing the target.
- [ ] Click through the mounted production component boundary rather than
      invoking its callback directly.
- [ ] Verify the owning hauling route becomes selected.
- [ ] Verify the selection indicator renders on the owning route header.
- [ ] Verify the map centers on the current stop with map-edge clamping.
- [ ] Verify the current stop becomes the native location highlight.
- [ ] Verify the Hauling menu remains open and native row data does not change.
- [ ] Move directly between two visible stop rows and prove the single rail
      rebinds without constructing another action set.
- [ ] Scroll the actual native list with the pointer over the list.
- [ ] Verify the rail moves, rebinds, or hides according to the row now under
      the pointer.
- [ ] Click after scrolling and prove the action targets the newly associated
      stop rather than the previous row occupant.
- [ ] Verify wheel input over the rail is consumed without changing map
      z-level or native scroll position.
- [ ] Close the Hauling menu and prove the rail disappears.
- [ ] Snapshot and restore viewport, z-level, highlight, pointer, route
      selection, list scroll, screen state, and every other changed UI value
      even when an assertion fails.
- [ ] Require DwarfSpec-confirmed cleanup with zero retained screens, subjects,
      pointer ownership, or mount state.

### Phase 6 completion gate

- [ ] Real native row hover renders the configured production rail wholly away
      from the native scrollbar.
- [ ] Pointer movement from row to rail preserves the current stop target.
- [ ] A real zoom-button click selects the correct route and centers and
      highlights the correct stop.
- [ ] Native scrolling changes or clears the target binding without stale
      activation.
- [ ] DwarfSpec reports a passing terminal run with confirmed cleanup.

## Phase 7: Validate reload, packaging, regressions, and live presentation

- [ ] Update `Docs/hover-action-rail-proposal.md` status after the production
      contract is proven.
- [ ] Mark the superseded permanent right-side placement requirements in
      `Docs/minecart-stop-row-actions.todo` and link to this checklist without
      rewriting historical evidence.
- [ ] Run focused hover-target, action, surface, placement, input, Hauling
      overlay, registry, and package-contract unit tests.
- [ ] Run the complete standalone unit suite.
- [ ] Run Lua syntax validation for production, unit, support, and `.ds.lua`
      files.
- [ ] Run the focused native-menu component test through
      `tools/Run-AutomationTests.ps1`.
- [ ] Keep `tools/Run-AutomationTests.ps1` as a direct pass-through to
      `dwarfspec run`.
- [ ] Run the complete DwarfSpec suite through the same pass-through wrapper.
- [ ] Run the offline build and inspect expanded package contents.
- [ ] Run archive package verification.
- [ ] Confirm the package contains the generic rail module and no obsolete
      per-row placement module that was intentionally removed.
- [ ] Run `git diff --check`.
- [ ] Install the packaged mod into the configured live DFHack instance.
- [ ] Run `dwarfui reload` and confirm exactly one registered route overlay and
      one hover rail instance exist.
- [ ] Confirm reload does not duplicate action widgets or retain an old
      callback generation.
- [ ] Visually confirm configured background, border, inset, and action
      alignment at several stop rows.
- [ ] Confirm the outer surface remains left of the panel at supported window
      sizes and interface scaling settings.
- [ ] Hover route headers, stop labels, stop numbers, rail borders, rail
      backgrounds, and action cells and confirm the expected ownership.
- [ ] Click stops on the current and other z-levels and confirm selection,
      centering, highlighting, marker rendering, and continued menu visibility.
- [ ] Scroll, edit routes, close and reopen the menu, disable and re-enable the
      overlay, and reload while checking for stale rails.
- [ ] Review the DFHack console and logs after rendering, hovering, clicking,
      scrolling, edits, closure, disable, and reload.
- [ ] Record unit, syntax, component, cleanup, package, reload, and manual live
      evidence separately.

### Phase 7 completion gate

- [ ] Focused and complete unit and DwarfSpec suites pass with zero failures.
- [ ] Syntax, offline build, expanded package, archive package, reload, and
      diff checks pass.
- [ ] Packaged live DFHack proves the rail presentation and interaction against
      the native Hauling menu.
- [ ] Reload and lifecycle operations leave no duplicate or stale rail state.
- [ ] Evidence distinguishes mocked logic, native component interaction,
      package contents, and manual visual confirmation.

## Definition of done

- [ ] `HoverActionTarget`, `HoverAction`, and `HoverActionRail` are documented,
      reusable production classes.
- [ ] The generic module imports no minecart or Hauling dependency.
- [ ] A rail constructs one action-widget set and reuses it across targets.
- [ ] Consumers can independently configure background, border, and inset.
- [ ] The full-parent controller remains visually transparent outside its
      moving surface.
- [ ] Hovering a target and moving onto the rail preserves action access.
- [ ] Leaving the retained region or losing context clears all transient state.
- [ ] Activation always uses a freshly validated target with the same key.
- [ ] Input ownership is deterministic for action, surface, target, scroll, and
      outside cells.
- [ ] The Hauling overlay shows one left-side rail only for the hovered stop.
- [ ] The rail never covers the native panel or scrollbar.
- [ ] Zoom activation selects the correct route and centers and highlights the
      current stop.
- [ ] Scrolling, edits, menu closure, disable, world unload, and reload cannot
      retain a stale target or callback.
- [ ] The obsolete permanent per-row placement system is no longer active.
- [ ] All new and modified classes and methods have complete LuaDoc comments.
- [ ] Unit, native component, cleanup, syntax, packaging, reload, and manual
      live evidence are recorded independently.

